    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nTotal radiant power emitted by Sun is P = 4π R_s² σ T⁴. This power spreads uniformly over a sphere of radius d at Earth's distance: Solar constant S = P / (4π d²) = σ T⁴ (R_s / d)².\n\n2. GIVEN:\nR_s = 7.0 × 10⁸ m, d = 1.656e+11 m, T = 5800 K, σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W/(m²·K⁴).\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nS = \\sigma T^4 \\left(\\frac{R_s}{d}\\right)^2\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Ratio (R_s / d) = (7.0 × 10⁸) / (1.656e+11) = 4.2271e-03.\n2. (R_s / d)² = 1.7868e-05.\n3. σ T⁴ = (5.67 × 10⁻⁸) × (5800)⁴ = 6.4165e+07 W/m².\n4. Solar constant S = 6.4165e+07 × 1.7868e-05 = 1146.5 W/m².\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: 1146.5 W/m²\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nThe solar constant on Earth is approximately 1360 to 1400 W/m² (or ~2 cal/(cm²·min))!",
